      "impact_statement": "shutil.unpack_archive() extracted ZIP members whose names are absolute Windows paths with a drive letter (such as C:\\...) outside the target directory, on Windows. Upstream fixed it in https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/146581 (3.13 commit https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/ab5ef98af693bded74a738570e81ea70abef2840), first released in CPython 3.13.14; see the advisory https://mail.python.org/archives/list/security-announce@python.org/thread/X6FXE5C6KDKOVNX3EC3DWD5RUPFWOZA4/ and https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-3087, which states that only Windows is affected. The escape depends on Windows path rules. These packages are Linux builds, where os.path is posixpath: a member name such as C:\\x or C:/x has no drive and is not absolute, so both the code before the fix and the fixed code extract it inside the target directory. In the DHI Alpine 3.23 python-3.13 package, 3.13.12-r0 to 3.13.13-r9 ship Lib/shutil.py unchanged from upstream CPython 3.13.12 and 3.13.13, without the fix; 3.13.14-r0 and later ship the upstream 3.13.14 or later file, which has it. Ran shutil.unpack_archive() with the interpreter from every published build on x86_64 and aarch64 (3.13.12-r0 to 3.13.18-r4) on a ZIP archive holding the members C:\\evil1.txt, C:/evil2.txt and D:\\dir\\evil3.txt: in every build all three were extracted inside the target directory (as files whose names contain the backslashes, or under a directory named C:), and nothing was written outside it."
