      "impact_statement": "CVE-2026-3087: shutil.unpack_archive() extracted ZIP members whose names are absolute Windows paths with a drive letter (such as C:\\...) outside the target directory on Windows. Upstream fixed it in https://github.com/python/cpython/issues/146581, on the 3.14 branch in commit https://github.com/python/cpython/commit/b01e594fbe754a960212f908d047294e880b52fd, first released in CPython 3.14.5; the advisory https://mail.python.org/archives/list/security-announce@python.org/thread/X6FXE5C6KDKOVNX3EC3DWD5RUPFWOZA4/ and https://www.cve.org/CVERecord?id=CVE-2026-3087 state that only Windows is affected. The escape depends on Windows path rules. These packages are Linux builds, where os.path is posixpath: a member name such as C:\\x or C:/x has no drive and is not absolute, so both the code before the fix and the fixed code extract it inside the target directory. We read the standard-library packages of every published DHI Debian 13 python-3.14 build, 24 builds from 3.14.5-0 to the current 3.14.10-7, on amd64 and arm64, each matching its repository index checksum: libpython-3.14-stdlib holds the pure-Python standard library up to 3.14.6-3 and libpython-3.14-minimal holds it from 3.14.6-4 on. Every build already carries the upstream fix in Lib/shutil.py and Lib/zipfile/__init__.py, on both architectures. Run with the interpreters from 3.14.5-5, 3.14.6-0 and 3.14.10-7 on amd64 and arm64, shutil.unpack_archive() on a ZIP archive extracted the members C:\\evil1.txt, C:/evil2.txt and D:\\dir\\evil3.txt inside the target directory (as files whose names contain the backslashes, or under a directory named C:) and wrote nothing outside it."
